\[
\text{Area of the larger circle} = \pi r_2^2 = \pi \cdot 2^2 = 4\pi
\]

The shaded region's area is given as \(\frac{5}{12}\) of the area of the larger circle:
\[
\text{Shaded area} = \frac{5}{12} \times 4\pi = \frac{5\pi}{3}
\]

The shaded region consists of two segments of the larger circle minus the corresponding segments of the smaller circle. Each segment is formed by the central angle \(\angle ADC\).

The area of one segment of the larger circle can be found by subtracting the area of the sector from the area of the triangle \(ADC\). The area of the sector of the larger circle with radius \(2\) and central angle \(\theta\) is:
\[
\text{Sector area} = \frac{\theta}{360^\circ} \times \pi \cdot 2^2 = \frac{\theta}{360^\circ} \times 4\pi = \frac{\theta \pi}{90^\circ}
\]

The area of the triangle \(ADC\) (which is an isosceles triangle with sides \(AD = 2\), \(DC = 2\), and \(AC = 2\sqrt{2}\)) can be calculated using the formula for the area of an isosceles triangle:
\[
\text{Area of } \triangle ADC = \frac{1}{2} \times AC \times \text{height}
\]
The height from \(D\) to \(AC\) can be found using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by splitting \(AC\) into two equal segments:
\[
\text{height} = \sqrt{2^2 - 1^2} = \sqrt{3}
\]
Thus,
\[
\text{Area of } \triangle ADC = \frac{1}{2} \times 2\sqrt{2} \times \sqrt{3} = \sqrt{6}
\]

